<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sharaf al-Dawla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/960">960</a>–<a href="/years/989">989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sharaf al-Dawla was a Buyid emir, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>. After his father's death in 983 he ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a>, then fought his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsam-al-dawla">Samsam al-Dawla</a> for the inheritance, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 987 and became senior emir of the dynasty. He died in 989 and was followed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baha-al-dawla">Baha al-Dawla</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sharaf-al-dawla.jpg" alt="Sharaf al-Dawla" width="320" height="151" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sharaf_al-Dawla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Before he took the title Sharaf al-Dawla he was known as Shirdil Abu'l-Fawaris. When his father '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> in 968, Shirdil was made viceroy of the province. For some time after 977 he lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> before being sent back to Kerman. As the eldest son he saw himself as his father's heir, and 'Adud al-Dawla's apparent preference for his second son Marzuban made him hostile to his brother. 'Adud al-Dawla had named no definite successor when he died in 983, and Marzuban, now called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsam-al-dawla">Samsam al-Dawla</a>, took power in Baghdad as senior amir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Shirdil pressed his own claim, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> and took the title Sharaf al-Dawla. He was soon surrounded by rivals: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsam-al-dawla">Samsam al-Dawla</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, his brothers Taj al-Dawla and Diya' al-Dawla in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a>, and Fakhr al-Dawla in Ray. He supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> when Fakhr al-Dawla tried to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> from them, yet the rulers of Basra and Khuzestan acknowledged Fakhr al-Dawla as senior amir, making him the most powerful of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>.</p><p>Sharaf al-Dawla nevertheless gained ground. He recovered Buyid Oman, which had gone over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsam-al-dawla">Samsam al-Dawla</a>, and early in 986 took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a>, driving his two brothers to Fakhr al-Dawla's lands. By May or June 986 Samsam al-Dawla recognised him as senior amir. Anarchy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> then drew him there. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> he deposed Samsam al-Dawla and imprisoned him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> with his official Fuladh ibn Manadhar, and had the Daylamite officer Ziyar ibn Shahrakawayh executed. In July 987 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> proclaimed him senior amir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His next aim was to subdue the Kurd Badr ibn Hasanwayh, an ally of Fakhr al-Dawla who had taken Diyarbakr during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsam-al-dawla">Samsam al-Dawla</a>'s rule, but the campaign failed, and he died not long afterwards. He had largely preserved his father's empire, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> of Ray under Fakhr al-Dawla had become effectively independent. After his death Samsam al-Dawla escaped from prison and seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sharaf al-Dawla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Adud al-Dawla's death in 983 his sons Samsam al-Dawla, <b>Sharaf al-Dawla</b> and Baha al-Dawla fought for the inheritance; Sharaf al-Dawla took Baghdad in 987, and Baha al-Dawla, ruling from 989 to 1012, reunited Iraq and Fars but could not restore Adud's authority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
